The reports identify gender inequality mainly through **gendered collaboration patterns**, disparities in **author recognition**, and unequal **editorial-board participation**, alongside broader institutional and national disparities. They do not specify the direction or size of these gaps.

Potential drivers include evaluation and ranking systems that rely on citations, author position, centrality, PageRank, or h-index; differences in visibility and dissemination; field and time effects; database coverage and disciplinary bias; and national research-evaluation policies. Peer-review reliability and evaluator reference standards may also affect recognition.

Measurement approaches include:

- large-scale Web of Science, Scopus, Dimensions, or similar bibliometric analyses;
- citation, usage, altmetric, and field-normalized indicators;
- fractional counting and author-name disambiguation;
- co-authorship, citation, and institutional-network analysis using centrality/PageRank;
- comparisons of bibliometric results with peer review and formal assessments;
- regression, longitudinal, clustering, and probabilistic methods.

The reports emphasize careful normalization, reproducibility, and attention to data-quality distortions.

